{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CfdPY3YyrSh6WvncmH2V7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe feature is placed sensibly — `assignHousehold`/`generateHouseholdId` sit next to the existing `rejectDuplicateHousehold`, `normalizeForComparison`, and `normalizeTelephone` helpers, mirroring the established controller pattern, and they reuse `normalizeForComparison` rather than re-implementing it. The model/schema/OpenAPI additions are all routine and consistent. The one structural problem is duplication:\n\n- **The household-membership predicate is now defined twice.** `rejectDuplicateHousehold` (OwnerRestControllerV1.java:384-386) and `assignHousehold` (OwnerRestControllerV1.java:410-412) both express the exact same \"normalized last name equals AND normalized address equals\" match over `clinicService.findAllOwners()`. One uses `.anyMatch(...)`, the other `.filter(...).toList()`, but the concept of \"who is in this household\" is defined in two places. If that matching rule ever changes (e.g. the spec's address-matching semantics are refined), a maintainer has to keep two copies in sync.\n\n  Extract a single helper, e.g.:\n  ```java\n  private List<Owner> findHouseholdMembers(String lastName, String address) {\n      String normalizedLastName = normalizeForComparison(lastName);\n      String normalizedAddress = normalizeForComparison(address);\n      return this.clinicService.findAllOwners().stream()\n          .filter(existing -> normalizeForComparison(existing.getLastName()).equals(normalizedLastName)\n              && normalizeForComparison(existing.getAddress()).equals(normalizedAddress))\n          .toList();\n  }\n  ```\n  Then `rejectDuplicateHousehold` becomes a check on `!findHouseholdMembers(...).isEmpty()` and `assignHousehold` consumes the returned list directly. This removes the duplicated predicate and makes the reject path and the assign path share one 